# Test Data Generation Rules

## Purpose

These rules define how future projects should generate fake, unique, deterministic, and safe test data.

The goal is to prevent inline random data in specs, avoid uncontrolled faker usage, and keep data generation ownership explicit under `src/test/data/**` once project implementation starts.

---

## Core Principle

Data generation is project-driven and explicit.

Do not add data-generation dependencies or abstractions before a real project need exists.

When needed, generators and builders belong to the project data layer, not specs.

---

## Starter Baseline Rule

The clean starter baseline ships with no project data layer and no `src/test/**` implementation beyond the **fixture skeleton** (`src/test/fixtures/**`).

Do not create `src/test/data/**`, security helpers, or reporting helpers until a concrete project needs them.

Do not install data-generation libraries in advance.

---

## Ownership And Location

When project implementation starts:

- generators belong under `src/test/data/generators/**`;
- builders belong under `src/test/data/builders/**`;
- reusable datasets belong under `src/test/data/datasets/**` when needed.

Do not place reusable data generation logic in specs, Page Objects, Components, API clients, or random helpers.

Do not duplicate the same reusable payload, form object, default profile, or shared constant across multiple specs, setup helpers, or builders.

---

## Duplication Policy

Before adding test data inline in a spec or setup helper, check whether the same shape already exists in:

- another spec;
- a setup helper such as `src/test/setup/**`;
- an existing builder;
- an existing dataset;
- another layer representation of the same entity.

### Must centralize

Move to the project data layer when the same data appears in two or more places:

- full valid API payloads;
- full valid UI form objects;
- shared default profiles for registration, login, contact, or other entities;
- shared passwords, dates of birth, and other reused auth defaults;
- shared invalid or boundary values used by both API and UI coverage;
- local spec helper functions that build reusable business data.

Preferred ownership:

```text
src/test/data/builders/**   -> valid structured payloads/forms by layer
src/test/data/datasets/**   -> shared constants and invalid/boundary values
src/test/data/generators/** -> unique primitive values such as email
src/test/setup/**           -> setup orchestration only; consume builders/datasets
```

### Keep local in specs

Allowed directly in specs:

- small deterministic scenario constants;
- one-off invalid overrides that are unique to one scenario;
- table-driven scenario metadata such as field names or expected error text;
- calls to approved builders, generators, and datasets from `src/test/data/**`.

### Layer split rule

When the same business entity has different contracts across layers, do not force one combined builder.

Use separate builders per layer and share only neutral constants through datasets.

Examples:

- API registration payload builder for snake_case request bodies;
- UI registration form builder for camelCase form data;
- shared auth defaults dataset for password and date-of-birth values.

Setup helpers such as disposable user registration must consume the API builder instead of owning a parallel payload object.

---

## Spec-Level Usage Rules

Do not call faker or random generators directly in specs.

Do not generate random data inline in tests.

Do not create builders for one-off deterministic values.

Allowed in specs:

- small deterministic scenario constants;
- calls to approved builders and generators from `src/test/data/**`.

---

## Builder Rules

Builders are for reusable structured domain data.

Builder defaults must be valid by default.

Invalid or negative data must be explicit through overrides.

Do not use builders for one-off constants or one-off deterministic payloads.

---

## Generator Rules

Generators are for primitive values such as:

- unique email suffixes;
- ids;
- timestamps;
- slugs.

Unique data should be deterministic enough to debug.

Include test-purpose prefixes when useful.

Avoid collisions in parallel execution.

---

## Faker Policy

`@faker-js/faker` is optional.

Prefer simple deterministic project-owned generators before adding faker or any external data generation dependency.

It may be added as a dev dependency only when a real project needs realistic fake data.

Before adding faker:

- confirm the project has a real need;
- verify current Node.js compatibility.

When faker is used:

- prefer seedable or deterministic configuration when repeatability is required;
- do not rely on uncontrolled randomness for assertions.

---

## Safety Rules For Generated Data

Do not generate fake emails, phones, addresses, or other data in ways that can trigger real external communication.

Prefer safe reserved domains for emails when possible, such as `example.test` or another project-approved non-routable domain.

If external email, SMS, payment, or other external systems are involved, generated data must be approved by the project plan.

Do not include real user data, secrets, or production identifiers in generated test data.

When generated data appears on screen, document mask or stable-state strategy in the feature plan (see Security and Sensitive Data Handling Rules).

Rule reference:

- `.cursor/rules/security-data-handling.rules.mdc`

---

## Uniqueness, Persistence, And Isolation

When generated data is persisted by API, UI, or E2E flows:

- define cleanup or safe isolation strategy;
- keep generated identifiers traceable for debugging;
- avoid shared mutable users or accounts by default.

Do not rely on shared mutable users/accounts unless explicitly approved by project planning.

---

## E2E Data Policy

E2E tests must use disposable or isolated data.

Destructive flows require cleanup or safe isolation.

Shared mutable users/accounts are forbidden unless explicitly approved.

Rule reference:

- `.cursor/rules/e2e-testing.rules.mdc`
- `.cursor/rules/test-isolation-state.rules.mdc`

---

## Multi-Target Ownership Policy

When data belongs to a specific UI app or API service, ownership must be documented in planning.

Do not use one generic generator for service-specific payloads without clear service ownership.

Rule reference:

- `.cursor/rules/multi-target-environment.rules.mdc`

---

## Review Expectations

Reviews should flag:

- faker or random usage directly in specs;
- non-deterministic data generation that harms repeatability;
- shared mutable users/accounts without explicit approval;
- builders created for one-off deterministic values;
- generated data that may affect real external systems;
- duplicated payload or form objects across specs;
- duplicated local `createValid...` helpers in specs;
- setup helpers that own parallel copies of builder-owned payloads;
- hardcoded shared passwords or invalid values repeated outside datasets.

---

## Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- installing faker before a real need exists;
- `Math.random()` or inline faker calls in specs;
- ad-hoc random emails and phones in tests;
- realistic fake data that can reach real external systems;
- one-off builders with no reuse;
- opaque unique values that are hard to debug;
- missing cleanup for persisted generated data;
- shared mutable test accounts in E2E by default;
- copy-pasting `base...Data` objects or `createValid...` helpers between specs;
- keeping full registration/login/contact payloads inline in specs when a builder exists;
- duplicating API setup payloads inside `src/test/setup/**` instead of reusing builders.

---

## Main Principle

Keep generated test data safe, deterministic, and owned by the project data layer.

No random data in specs.

No preinstalled faker by default.
